From 7be039d33bcd8979bf0d73ad5e532b2aabf03e83 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: moWerk <mo@mowerk.net>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:18:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qcom: q6afe: real-time proxy ports as voice back ends
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The AFE real-time proxy ports (0x2000 RX, 0x2001 TX) are the DSP's host
data path: what the DSP plays into RX the host can read, what the host
writes the DSP captures from TX. Downstream uses them to carry a call's
audio to and from the host, where it goes to a Bluetooth headset over
HCI. This adds them as ordinary back ends, gate 1 of that work:

- port indices RT_PROXY_RX_1/RT_PROXY_TX_1, port table entries and the
  RT proxy config (16-bit interleaved, frame = period, jitter half a
  frame, no watermark events, as downstream configures them);
- q6afe-dai DAIs, widgets and routes, started and stopped like the
  codec DMA ports;
- q6voice-dai: "RT_PROXY_TX_1" in the VoiceMMode1 capture mixer and an
  "RT_PROXY_RX_1 Voice Mixer";
- the Pro1-X board: two back-end links with stand-in S/PDIF codecs.

The host side (shared memory, read and write commands) comes with the
next change. Built, not yet run on the device.

Disclosure: LLMGD-2 · origin O1 (the model wrote the change from the downstream audio-kernel sources)
LLMGD: v0.3; assurance=A2; flags=none; origin=O1; origin_headline=O1; scope=kernel; graded-by=claude-opus-5-5; retrieval=none
